          "The symbol concealed unequal attention. Sailors were often poor, mobile, foreign-born, or difficult to locate in a stable household record. Political leaders who invoked them did not thereby commit themselves to a broad program of labor rights. Enslaved people received no comparable presumption that the flag protected their liberty. Native nations defending land were described as instruments of Britain more readily than as polities making claims. The republic's indignation was selective even when the maritime injury was genuine.",
